Dermatomyositis
Rheumatology & Immunology
Heliotrope rash, Gottron's papules, Symmetrical proximal muscle weakness

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RHI-011
Reason for Visit: Rapid, progressive inability to stand from a seated position or climb stairs, accompanied by a new facial rash and profound dysphagia, requiring urgent physical assessment to rule out acute bulbar involvement or respiratory muscle failure.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a rapidly worsening symmetrical proximal weakness, now complicated by difficulty swallowing (dysphagia). Urgent after-hours bedside assessment was strictly required to evaluate for imminent airway compromise, assess the severity of myositis, and determine if immediate emergency hospital transfer was necessary for intravenous immunosuppression and airway protection, as delaying until standard hours carried a severe risk of aspiration or respiratory arrest.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I can't get out of this chair without my husband pulling me up, my throat feels weak when I swallow water, and my eyes are swollen and purple."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a 4-week insidious history of progressive, symmetrical proximal muscle weakness, initially noticed as difficulty climbing stairs. Over the past 48 hours, this weakness has acutely worsened, rendering the patient unable to rise from a seated position independently. Crucially, the patient reports new-onset dysphagia today, coughing when attempting to drink liquids, due to suspected oropharyngeal muscle involvement. Concurrently, a violaceous periorbital edema (Heliotrope rash) has erupted. Also notes a photosensitive macular erythema on the neck and chest (Shawl sign).
Functional Status: Currently confined to a chair; completely dependent for transfers and mobilization.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of aspiration pneumonia and falls; spouse is struggling with physical transfers.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/82 mmHg | HR: 98 bpm | Temp: 37.3 C | RR: 20 bpm | SpO2: 96% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Patient appears anxious, sitting rigidly, speech is slightly nasal. Bilateral periorbital swelling with a distinct purplish hue is prominent.
Physical Exam: Neurological/Musculoskeletal: Profound symmetrical proximal muscle weakness. Deltoid and hip flexor power is 2/5 bilaterally. Distal strength (grip, ankle dorsiflexion) is preserved at 5/5. Neck flexor weakness is profound (patient unable to lift head against gravity while supine). Dermatological: Classic Heliotrope rash present. Gottron's papules (violaceous, scaly eruptions) are clearly visible over the metacarpophalangeal and interphalangeal joints bilaterally. Mechanics hands noted with hyperkeratotic, fissured palmar skin. Periungual erythema and telangiectasias present. GI/Oropharyngeal: Gag reflex is present but patient exhibits a delayed, effortful swallow with a sip of water, coughing immediately after. Diagnostic tetrad integration shows classical Heliotrope rash, Gottron's papules, and profound symmetrical proximal muscle weakness, heavily predicting elevated serum creatine kinase (CK) and positive anti-Mi-2 or anti-Jo-1 antibodies upon formal testing.
Environmental Observations: No specialized seating or transfer equipment available in the home.
Medication Reconciliation: No recent myotoxic medications (e.g., high-dose statins) identified.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Dermatomyositis - Status: Exacerbated (Acute bulbar/proximal crisis)
Differentials:

Myasthenia Gravis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of defining, classic cutaneous stigmata (Gottron's, Heliotrope) and the continuous rather than fatigable nature of the profound proximal weakness.

Polymyalgia Rheumatica - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of profound objective motor weakness (rather than purely pain/stiffness) and the presence of bulbar symptoms and rash.

Statin-induced Necrotizing Myopathy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of statin exposure, presence of classic pathognomonic rashes, and prominent bulbar involvement.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Aspiration and mobility failure)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute exacerbation of severe, symmetrical proximal myopathy now critically involving the oropharyngeal musculature, as evidenced by clinical dysphagia and cough. Combined with the pathognomonic cutaneous signs, this is a severe presentation of Dermatomyositis. The emergence of bulbar weakness places the patient at immediate, life-threatening risk for aspiration pneumonia or acute ventilatory failure from diaphragmatic involvement. An urgent after-hours bedside assessment was absolutely mandated to formally test swallow safety, assess respiratory effort, and trigger an immediate emergency hospital admission. Leaving the patient unassessed overnight posed an unacceptable mortality risk, perfectly validating MBS 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: The patient is clinically unsafe to remain in the community due to severe dysphagia and profound proximal weakness. Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department via ambulance is required for urgent admission, CPK/aldolase testing, autoantibody screening, formal speech pathology swallow assessment, and initiation of high-dose intravenous corticosteroids and potential IVIG.
Positive Behavioral Change: Strict Nil By Mouth (NBM) protocol: Completely ceasing all oral intake (food, water, medications) immediately; explicitly linking this absolute restriction to the prevention of fluid entering the lungs (aspiration) while waiting for the ambulance. Postural maintenance: Remaining strictly upright at a 90-degree angle in the chair; achieving this position actively utilizes gravity to keep oral secretions from pooling in the airway.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None; acute admission required.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Acute hospital admission.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance transfer organized. If the patient begins to choke on their own saliva, becomes acutely short of breath, or loses consciousness while waiting, dial 000 immediately and update the priority.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The combination of your rash and profound muscle weakness, especially the swallowing difficulty, strongly indicates a severe inflammatory muscle disease that requires immediate hospital treatment.
Clinical Course: With aggressive intravenous treatment, the inflammation in the muscles can be halted, but recovery of strength is slow and requires prolonged rehabilitation and medication.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to eat or drink anything, not even a sip of water, as your swallowing muscles are failing and fluid will go into your lungs. Wait for the ambulance in a fully upright seated position.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your immune system is aggressively attacking your own muscles, including the ones you use to swallow and breathe. You need an immediate hospital admission for strong intravenous steroids to stop this attack and keep your airway safe.
Actionable Steps: Do not swallow any food or liquid. Stay sitting upright. Go directly to the emergency department with the paramedics.

